{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 2 complete — fix landed for review as Gerrit change 1512\n\n**RED → GREEN, held-out oracle.**\n\nThe RED oracle is `tests/unit/test_review_bot.py::test_reconcile_cursor_holds_back_an_abandoned_non_newest_candidate` plus three siblings and `tests/unit/test_review_bot_in_flight.py::test_health_endpoint_reports_the_queue_depth`. All were observed failing for the right reason before any fix existed (the root-cause oracle failed on its own assertion message: *\"the abandoned candidate fell outside the next window\"*). The tests were then physically removed from the working tree and the fix was written by a subagent that never saw them — it received only the contract card. On re-application: **97 passed**.\n\n**Mutation check (the test has teeth).** Re-introducing the exact confirmed defect — dropping the low-water mark so the cursor advances unconditionally to `newest` — returned the oracle to RED with that same message, and only that test. Restored after.\n\n**Refactoring litmus.** Every assertion is on an observable: which change ids get re-driven, the persisted cursor file's contents, the `reconcile_done` JSON, the `/health` body. No private names, no source-text greps.\n\n### The fix\n\nThe cursor is now a **low-water mark**: a pass persists `min(newest_event, oldest_retryably_abandoned_candidate)`. Because `?t1=` is inclusive, clamping *to* the abandoned event's own time is enough to re-admit it.\n\nThe partition is explicit over the voter's real status vocabulary, because \"not voted\" and \"retryable\" are different sets — a point the plan-review gate blocked on, correctly:\n* **HOLD** — `error` (all stages), `deferred`, plus the reconciler's own `reconcile_check_error` and `review_timeout`.\n* **ADVANCE** — `voted`, and every terminal `skipped` reason including `post_vote_closed`. Holding that one back would re-drive a merged/abandoned change into a 409 forever (bug c943) and grow the window without bound.\n\nBounded by `RECONCILE_MAX_HOLDBACK_SECONDS` (24h) so a permanently-failing candidate cannot pin the cursor; releasing one emits `RECONCILE_DEGRADED reason=holdback_expired`.\n\n### Replication — the original report no longer reproduces\n\nThe Phase 1 reproduction script, unchanged, re-run against the fixed code:\n\n```\nBEFORE                                              AFTER\nPASS 1: window=[Iaaa,Ibbb,Iccc] scanned:2 rev:1     PASS 1: window=[Iaaa,Ibbb,Iccc] scanned:2 rev:1\nPASS 2: window=[Iccc]           scanned:0 rev:0     PASS 2: window=[Iaaa,Ibbb,Iccc] scanned:2 rev:1\nPASS 3: window=[Iccc]           scanned:0 rev:0     PASS 3: window=[Iaaa,Ibbb,Iccc] scanned:2 rev:1\n\nun-voted candidate in later windows: False          un-voted candidate in later windows: True\n```\n\nThe abandoned candidate is now re-driven on every subsequent pass, and `RECONCILE_DEGRADED` fires each time — so a genuinely stuck change is loud rather than silent.\n\n### AC5 — the three states are now distinguishable\n\n| state | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| bot down | `/health` unreachable | unchanged |\n| review running | `in_flight` | unchanged |\n| queued behind N | **invisible** | `/health` `queue_depth` |\n| backfill carrying a stalled change | **invisible** | `reconcile_done` `held_back` + `cursor` |\n| chronically stuck | **invisible** | `RECONCILE_DEGRADED reason=holdback_expired` |\n\n### Scope and safety\n\n* Caller sweep: `reconcile_once` / `_write_cursor` are reached only from `reconcile_loop` and tests (the many `reconcile_once` grep hits are the unrelated `_engine/rebar_reconciler`). No caller depends on the lossy behaviour; the change can only ADD recovery. Idempotency is untouched — the dedup ledger and the authoritative `has_llm_review_vote` pre-filter still make a re-drive a no-op.\n* Restoration, not creation: the reconciler was always contracted to re-drive an abandoned candidate. `queue_depth` / `held_back` are this ticket's own AC5.\n* No new dependency (no new imports in `src/`).\n* No test weakened: the only deleted test line is the double's `return list(self._events)`, replaced by a `since`-honouring filter — a strictly stronger fake. No assertion removed, no `skip`/`xfail` added.\n* Gates: `make lint` clean, `make typecheck` clean (437 files), **9631 passed / 1 pre-existing generated-doc staleness** which the fix's new env var caused and which `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py` resolved.\n\n### Siblings (Step 7 sweep, both axes)\n\n* **Construct axis** — swept every persisted resume position in the repo. Most are safe by construction (`autodeploy.sh`'s `deployed-sha` is gated on success; the GET-rotation stamp deprioritizes rather than excludes; the Jira reconciler's `prev_snapshot.json` is edge-triggered but backstopped by a level-triggered `binding_walk`). **One true sibling**: `infra/scripts/observability.sh:208-212` (and four sibling sites) advances its metric offset BEFORE the CloudWatch publish, so a transient `put-metric-data` failure permanently loses that interval's count — including `voter_errors`, the metric that alarms on this very gate. Filed as **6a65** (`prettying-phantastic-goldfish`), linked `discovered_from`.\n* **Parity axis** — the rule is *\"a recovery path for a stuck change must not permanently drop the request on a transient failure.\"* `/webhook`, `_worker` and `reconcile_loop` hold. **MISSING**: `/rerun` (`app.py:367-375`) and `retrigger.handle_comment_added` (`retrigger.py:344-361`) are fire-and-forget into the volatile queue, and the reconciler cannot back them up because its `has_llm_review_vote` pre-filter excludes a change that already carries a vote — which is exactly the stuck-at-`-1` case both exist to fix. `retrigger` additionally burns the retry-budget re-arm and posts \"a fresh review has been queued\" before the event can evaporate. Filed as **b0f5** (`smashable-fameless-godwit`), linked `discovered_from`. No CLI surface exists for the review bot at all.\n\n### Residual risk\n\n* The `WORKER_COUNT = 1` queue lane is at ρ≈1.05 during a landing burst (21 patchset-created/hour against a ~3-minute review). That is a real capacity constraint and an amplifier, but it violates no written contract and is not this bug — **accepted**, and now at least visible via `queue_depth`.\n* `app.py:73-74` justifies the single worker with \"reviews serialize on the per-(change,rev) lock anyway\", which is false — the lock is keyed by the pair, so distinct changes would run concurrently. Left in place: changing worker concurrency is a capacity decision, not a bug fix. **Accepted** (noted here so the next reader does not trust the comment).\n\nNow landing as https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1512 — which is itself the end-to-end test of the reported trigger path, on the still-unfixed deployed bot."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786122116969392001,"uuid":"7f8bd4cb-23ae-42fa-82fd-edc71fcb2911"}